pilot=f'''<h2>Earn the next market.</h2><p class="lead">Start with Austin: two billboard faces, four weeks of media, one controlled field experience, and one measurable recruiting journey.</p>
<div class="grid4" style="margin-top:34px"><div class="metric rule"><span class="eyebrow">Primary outcome</span><b>Talent</b><p>Qualified candidates for a signed-off list of Texas roles.</p></div><div class="metric rule"><span class="eyebrow">Recommended scope</span><b>1 city</b><p>Austin first. Boston has its own later decision and budget.</p></div><div class="metric rule"><span class="eyebrow">Media flight</span><b>4 weeks</b><p>Both faces run together. Full branding from day one.</p></div><div class="metric rule"><span class="eyebrow">Planning envelope</span><b>$104–179k</b><p>Rounded range including 15% contingency. Exact total: {money(ATL)}–{money(ATH)}.</p></div></div>
<div class="grid2" style="margin-top:30px"><div><h3>What ships</h3><ul><li>Two standard Austin bulletins with one headline and a consistent official wordmark.</li><li>A one-day, eight-hour demonstration at a controlled site, with one machine and one console.</li><li>A recruiting destination with current roles, Texas location details, and campaign attribution.</li><li>Approved field footage, employee stories, and a focused distribution flight.</li></ul></div><div><h3>What unlocks Boston</h3><p>Review the Austin funnel at 30, 60, and 90 days. Proceed only when recruiting confirms role fit, marketing can explain acquisition cost, and engineering and operations can support another event.</p><p>Boston is the first candidate expansion for robotics and systems talent. Its timing follows the evidence; its spend is not included in Austin. The other eight cities remain a creative library.</p></div></div>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Decision proposed:</strong> authorize quote development against the Austin scope, with a planning ceiling of {money(ATH)}. Up to $32,000 of that envelope covers creative and the recruiting journey. Release media and event commitments only after inventory, landing-page, operating, and permit gates pass. No media or vendors are booked by this plan.</p></div>'''

system='''<h2>One world. Clear words.</h2><p class="lead">Keep the chrome Operator, bone canvas, Mars orange, and working machinery. Let the message change by audience; let the brand stay recognizable.</p>
<div class="grid2"><div><h3>Neon with a solid core</h3><p>Every headline now uses solid orange letterforms, a dark defining edge, and a restrained outer glow. The words remain intact when the glow disappears. Script and hollow neon lettering are removed.</p><p>Headlines are vector paths rather than generated image text. The official TerraFirma wordmark, action line, and web address are also vectors, so they remain crisp at larger sizes.</p><p>The standard master is 48:14. Keep every line within the central clear area; do not place lettering over the Operator, excavator, grid, or horizon.</p></div><div><h3>A consistent crew</h3><p><strong>Austin:</strong> the Texas cowboy character, with hat and scarf.<br><strong>Engineering markets:</strong> plain technical workwear.<br><strong>Field and infrastructure markets:</strong> a plain hardhat and work jacket.</p><p>Boston loses the scally cap and shamrock. Nashville loses the rhinestone treatment. Other markets lose novelty accessories, invented badges, and visual cues that imply an affiliation.</p><p>Local relevance comes from the work and the headline. The same three character treatments carry the entire system.</p></div></div>
<div class="palette"><span style="background:#EFEBE4">BONE<br>#EFEBE4</span><span style="background:#14182A;color:#EFEBE4">INK<br>#14182A</span><span style="background:#E76424">NEON CORE<br>#E76424</span><span style="background:#B4583A;color:white">MARS<br>#B4583A</span><span style="background:#8E949C">CHROME<br>#8E949C</span></div>
<details><summary>Production and readability gates</summary><div class="grid2"><div><ul><li>Three-second comprehension check with people outside the project: headline, TerraFirma, and Texas hiring invitation.</li><li>Test a reduced proof and the actual media-owner sightline at its expected viewing distance, speed, and lighting.</li><li>Require the headline, wordmark, and URL to remain legible with glow switched off. Keep glow outside the letters; preserve counters and spacing.</li><li>Have the printer proof orange, dark edges, fine illustration detail, safe area, and material under day and night conditions.</li></ul></div><div><ul><li>These are creative masters, not signed-off mechanical files. Recompose for each vendor’s dimensions and safe zones; do not stretch the board.</li><li>Keep one headline, one brand, one action, and one short URL. No QR code on high-speed road inventory.</li><li>Miami needs fluent Spanish review and a matching bilingual recruiting path before launch.</li><li>Illustration establishes the world. Approved photography and product demonstrations supply the evidence on the destination and in follow-up content.</li></ul></div></div></details>'''

city_content='<h2>Ten cities.<br>One campaign language.</h2><p class="lead">Austin is the recommended buy. Boston is the next candidate. Every other board is an adaptable future concept, with no media commitment.</p><p class="note">Tap a billboard to open its full-size vector master. All ten use the same readable neon treatment.</p><div class="city-nav">'+''.join(f'<a href="#{c["id"]}">{c["name"]}</a>' for c in CITIES)+'</div>'
for c in CITIES:
    city_content+=f'''<article class="city" id="{c['id']}"><div class="city-head"><h3>{escape(c['name'])}</h3><span class="status {'pilot' if c['id']=='austin' else ''}">{c['status']}</span></div>{board(c)}<div class="caption"><span>48:14 concept master · {c['family'].title()} character</span><a href="downloads/billboard-{c['id']}.svg" download>Download SVG ↗</a></div><div class="city-grid"><div><p><strong>{' '.join(c['lines'])}</strong></p><p>{c['reason']}</p></div><dl><dt>Audience</dt><dd>{c['role']}</dd><dt>Placement</dt><dd>{c['placement']}</dd><dt>Proof</dt><dd>{c['proof']}</dd></dl></div><a class="back" href="#cities">↑ All city concepts</a></article>'''

experience='''<h2>Let people see<br>the work happen.</h2><p class="lead">A controlled Mission Control experience connects the future-facing campaign to real engineering and field operations.</p>
<div class="grid4 flow" style="margin-top:38px"><div class="rule"><h3>Book</h3><p>Choose a timed slot. State whether the session is a live demonstration, supervised participation, or a simulator.</p></div><div class="rule"><h3>Brief</h3><p>Explain the task, the human-in-the-loop system, and the boundaries. A qualified operator retains control authority.</p></div><div class="rule"><h3>Experience</h3><p>Watch or complete one approved task in a segregated practice area. No public access to active customer work.</p></div><div class="rule"><h3>Connect</h3><p>Meet a builder or engineer, see relevant roles, and choose whether to receive recruiting follow-up.</p></div></div>
<div class="grid2" style="margin-top:24px"><div><h3>One machine, honest capacity</h3><p>Planning model: one machine × 60 minutes ÷ six minutes per slot × 70% operating availability = <strong>seven sessions per hour, or 56 over eight hours</strong>. The slot includes briefing, activity, and reset. Validate this in rehearsal before releasing appointments.</p><p>Staffing breaks, faults, weather, and resets reduce availability. Use timed bookings and a waitlist. Do not promise a zero queue. A second console on the same machine does not double capacity.</p></div><div><h3>Clear demonstration boundaries</h3><p>Engineering and EHS approve the machine, control path, stop behavior, exclusion area, supervision, and weather limits. Do not substitute a broadcast video link for a validated machine-control connection.</p><p>If public operation is not approved, run an operator-led demonstration or clearly labeled simulator. A collectible mission card is a souvenir, not a license, certification, or hiring assessment.</p></div></div>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Austin format:</strong> one local site, one machine, one console, one day. The pilot budget includes setup, external crew, local logistics, and an operating allowance. A container tour, multi-city playgrounds, and additional pods require separately costed proposals.</p></div>
<details><summary>Run of show and operating ownership</summary><ul><li><strong>Before opening:</strong> producer signs off access, site approvals, accessibility, weather decision, power, and connectivity; engineering and EHS complete the operating rehearsal and stop test.</li><li><strong>During the eight-hour program:</strong> venue host manages timed entry; the qualified operator and safety lead manage each approved session; engineering provides technical support; recruiters hold separate career conversations. Confirm named people and relief coverage in the staffing roster.</li><li><strong>At close:</strong> secure machinery, reconcile incidents and downtime, capture actual costs, and check opt-in records. Use booking contact details for service messages only unless the attendee separately opts into marketing.</li><li><strong>Within two business days:</strong> recruiting follows up with consenting, relevant candidates. Publish only approved footage, with releases where required.</li></ul></details>'''

budget=f'''<h2>A budget that matches<br>the proposed pilot.</h2><p class="lead">Austin: {money(ATL)}–{money(ATH)}, including a consistent 15% contingency. The working midpoint is {money((ATL+ATH)/2)}.</p><p>These are new planning allowances in USD, not vendor quotes or commitments. Quantities are explicit. Replace allowances with written quotes before purchase; keep a separate record of deposits, actuals, and internal time.</p>
{budget_table(AUSTIN)}
<div class="grid2" style="margin-top:28px"><div><h3>Scope and accounting</h3><p>Media is two faces for four weeks. The field experience is one Austin day. Creative, the destination, production, external crew, logistics, capture, distribution, and measurement are all in the same envelope.</p><p>Each quote must include applicable tax, agency or booking fees, delivery, installation, removal, damage coverage, and required deposits. Reforecast the total before signing if a quote excludes them.</p></div><div><h3>Resources still to confirm</h3><p>Internal salaries, recruiting time, engineering opportunity cost, and depreciation of owned equipment are excluded from this cash view. Finance records them separately so the full resource cost stays visible.</p><p>The machine / console allowance covers incremental use or rental and integration, not new hardware acquisition. If compatible hardware is unavailable, re-scope the demo before committing; do not hide a purchase in contingency.</p></div></div>
<details><summary>Optional Boston expansion · {money(BTL)}–{money(BTH)} additional</summary><p>Separate four-week media flight and one technical recruiting event. Not included in Austin and not pre-approved. Reuse creative and the landing journey; no national tour or premium landmark buy.</p>{budget_table(BOSTON)}<p class="note">Austin plus Boston, if both later authorized: {money(ATL+BTL)}–{money(ATH+BTH)}. Do not scale either city’s average into a ten-city forecast.</p></details>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Single source of truth:</strong> this itemization and its downloadable CSV replace the previous Pilot / Core / Flagship estimates for this recommendation. Earlier Google budget sheets and vendor notes are not the active basis for authorization. There is no implied ten-city baseline.</p></div><a class="button" href="downloads/terrafirma-pilot-budget.csv" download>Download itemized budget CSV ↗</a>'''

timeline='''<h2>Prepare first.<br>Launch when ready.</h2><p class="lead">Target Austin media for March 15–April 11, 2027: four complete weeks. Preparation starts now; the evaluation window continues after the media ends.</p>
<dl class="timeline"><dt>Nov–Dec 2026</dt><dd><p><strong>Define and quote.</strong> Recruiting selects priority roles and baseline performance. Brand finalizes the system. Media buyer gets two-face inventory quotes. Producer confirms a feasible site and the applicable permit path immediately.</p></dd><dt>January 2027</dt><dd><p><strong>Build and validate.</strong> Complete the landing journey, tracking, print proofs, operating plan, and named roster. Engineering validates the demonstration. Review current inventory and all-in quotes.</p></dd><dt>January 29</dt><dd><p><strong>Launch gate.</strong> Brand, recruiting, finance, engineering, and EHS agree that the creative, destination, venue, approvals, and operating plan are ready. If the permit schedule cannot support March, move the flight and event together; do not compress required lead times.</p></dd><dt>February</dt><dd><p><strong>Produce and rehearse.</strong> Confirm vendor specifications, print and install dates, release capture plans, and test the complete booking-to-recruiting journey. Release session capacity only after the timed rehearsal.</p></dd><dt>Mar 15–Apr 11</dt><dd><p><strong>Run the four-week flight.</strong> Launch both fully branded faces together. Hold one field experience during the flight on the approved date. Optimize the destination and follow-up without changing the experiment’s core scope.</p></dd><dt>May 11 / Jun 10 / Jul 10</dt><dd><p><strong>Review at 30 / 60 / 90 days after the flight.</strong> Track qualified candidates through screens, offers, and acceptance. Retention is measured 90 days after each hire’s own start date. Decide whether Boston deserves the next investment.</p></dd><dt>After July review</dt><dd><p><strong>Potential Boston expansion.</strong> Set a new flight only after the hiring brief, evidence, quotes, staffing, and venue are approved. Allow the actual production and booking lead time. No April Boston event is assumed in this plan.</p></dd></dl>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Calendar reality:</strong> SXSW is March 15–21, 2027, but this plan has no festival affiliation or reserved inventory. Austin permit lead times depend on event classification; some paths may already rule out the March target. A producer must resolve that before any nonrefundable commitment.</p></div>
<details><summary>Future event calendar — context, not a tour schedule</summary><p>Published 2027 Tech Week dates: Boston, April 26–May 2; New York, June 7–13; Los Angeles, September 13–19; San Francisco, October 4–10. Tech Week consists of independently hosted events across each city; there is no assumed central booth.</p><p>Boston’s April event precedes the pilot’s 90-day review and is therefore outside this recommendation. The remaining dates do not fit a single eight-week national tour. Any later market needs its own timing and budget.</p></details>'''

measurement='''<h2>Measure the invitation.<br>Follow it to a hire.</h2><div class="grid2"><div><h3>Before the first impression</h3><p>Recruiting defines the roles, qualification criteria, target seniority, on-site requirements, and response owner. Establish the prior 90-day funnel and a comparable market or channel where practical.</p><p>Agree on a maximum cost per qualified screen using actual recruiting economics. Set a minimum cohort size and a follow-up service level before launch. Do not manufacture a hire forecast from billboard impressions.</p></div><div><h3>One connected journey</h3><p>Use the short company URL on the board. Before launch, make the campaign and current roles easy to find from that homepage; track the campaign destination, event links, and role handoff.</p><p>The current public careers route is TerraFirma’s Ashby board. Any dedicated /careers or campaign redirect must be created and tested by TerraFirma before printing a new address. State Texas work location and only confirmed relocation terms.</p></div></div>
<div class="grid4" style="margin-top:24px"><div class="rule"><h3>Recall</h3><p>Unaided brand and message recall in a small matched pre/post sample. Report sample size and uncertainty.</p></div><div class="rule"><h3>Interest</h3><p>Relevant visits, returning visitors, role views, and demo bookings. Separate media traffic from event and paid digital traffic.</p></div><div class="rule"><h3>Quality</h3><p>Qualified applications, completed screens, interview progression, and cost per qualified screen.</p></div><div class="rule"><h3>Outcome</h3><p>Offers, acceptances, starts, and 90-day retention by cohort. Track first-touch and assisted source alongside self-report.</p></div></div>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Scale rule:</strong> Boston proceeds only if the Austin cohort is sufficiently large to evaluate, role quality meets recruiting’s agreed bar, acquisition cost is within the pre-agreed ceiling, and follow-up and event delivery work reliably. If evidence is weak or incomplete, improve or extend the test before expanding.</p></div>
<p class="note">Billboard exposure is not individually attributable. Use vendor audience estimates as estimates, triangulate with the baseline and comparison, and avoid presenting assisted hires or direct traffic as proven incremental lift.</p>'''

execution='''<h2>Make the next decision<br>concrete.</h2><div class="grid3"><div class="rule"><h3>Brand + recruiting</h3><p>Approve the campaign story, the Austin headline, the official identity, and the role brief. Finish the destination and real-world proof. Confirm the three-second read before mechanical artwork.</p></div><div class="rule"><h3>Producer + engineering</h3><p>Return a feasible venue, permit path, machine and console scope, staffing roster, operating plan, and rehearsal results. Resolve the March timing before reserving nonrefundable inventory.</p></div><div class="rule"><h3>Finance + leadership</h3><p>Review itemized, all-in quotes against the Austin envelope. Authorize staged commitments, name the decision owner, and confirm the criteria that would justify Boston.</p></div></div>
<details><summary>Vendor brief and quote requirements</summary><ul><li><strong>Media owner:</strong> face IDs, exact locations, dimensions, sightline photos, dates, audience methodology, posting and removal charges, cancellation terms, and makegoods.</li><li><strong>Printer:</strong> material, color proof, safe area, weather durability, production lead time, delivery, and installation responsibility.</li><li><strong>Venue and producer:</strong> lawful use, accessibility, power, barriers, security, approved capacity, weather fallback, crew roster, permit responsibilities, and insurance requirements.</li><li><strong>Equipment and engineering:</strong> compatibility, integration scope, machine availability, transport, control-link requirements, stop behavior, supervision, and a tested fallback.</li><li><strong>Web and recruiting:</strong> working role links, location and relocation copy, source capture, consent handling, analytics, mobile QA, and a named follow-up owner.</li></ul><p class="note">Vendor categories are briefs, not recommendations or confirmed partners. Replace each allowance with a comparable written quote and record the date, validity, inclusions, and exclusions.</p></details>'''

sources=[('TerraFirma — mission, solutions, and Texas project examples','https://terrafirma.inc/'),('TerraFirma — long-term mission','https://terrafirma.inc/our-mission/'),('TerraFirma — current roles','https://jobs.ashbyhq.com/TerraFirma-Inc'),('SXSW — official March 15–21, 2027 dates','https://sxsw.com/2026/south-by-southwest-2027-dates-announced/'),('Tech Week — 2027 dates and decentralized format','https://www.tech-week.com/about'),('Austin Center for Events — event planning and permit guidance','https://www.austintexas.gov/ace/event-planning-guide')]

downloads='''<h2>Take the plan with you.</h2><div class="downloads"><a class="download" href="downloads/terrafirma-full-book.pdf"><b>Campaign plan ↗</b><span>The revised story, city concepts, pilot, itemized budget, timeline, and decision gates.</span></a><a class="download" href="downloads/terrafirma-billboards-edition.pdf"><b>Billboards edition ↗</b><span>All ten revised boards, with clear pilot / expansion / future-concept labels.</span></a><a class="download" href="downloads/terrafirma-pilot-budget.csv" download><b>Budget CSV ↗</b><span>Quantities, unit allowances, extended costs, and 15% contingency for Austin and optional Boston.</span></a></div><p class="note" style="margin-top:20px">The site, PDFs, SVG masters, and CSV are generated from the same campaign source. Revision 3 supersedes the earlier campaign recommendation.</p>'''

head=f'<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><meta name="robots" content="noindex,nofollow"><meta name="description" content="TerraFirma campaign plan: Texas proof, a Mars horizon, readable billboard creative, and a focused Austin pilot."><title>TerraFirma — Texas Born, Mars Bound</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="campaign.css"></head>'
header='''<header class="nav"><div class="wrap nav-inner"><a class="brand" href="#top" aria-label="TerraFirma campaign home"><img src="img/v3/official-logo-1.svg" alt="TerraFirma" width="1164" height="144"></a><nav aria-label="Campaign navigation"><a href="#story">Story</a><a href="#pilot">Pilot</a><a href="#cities">Billboards</a><a href="#budget">Budget</a><a href="#timeline">Timeline</a><a href="#downloads">Downloads</a></nav></div></header>'''
hero=f'''<section class="hero" id="top"><div class="wrap"><span class="eyebrow">Campaign plan / {VERSION}</span><div class="hero-top"><h1>Texas Born.<br><em>Mars Bound.</em></h1><div><p>Build the future from the ground up. A campaign grounded in Texas work, with the ambition to help humanity build on Mars.</p><p><strong>The recommendation: prove the campaign in Austin, then earn the next market.</strong></p><div class="buttons"><a class="button" href="#pilot">Review the pilot ↓</a><a class="button" href="#cities">Explore the billboards ↓</a></div></div></div><div class="hero-art">{board(CITIES[0])}</div><div class="hero-foot"><span>Austin first · 2 faces · 4 weeks · 1 controlled field experience</span><span>Planning proposal · No media or vendors booked</span></div></div></section>'''
sections=[('01','Brand story',story,'story',''),('02','Recommended pilot',pilot,'pilot','alt'),('03','Visual and copy system',system,'system',''),('04','Billboard concepts',city_content,'cities','alt'),('05','Field experience',experience,'experience','dark'),('06','Budget',budget,'budget',''),('07','Timeline',timeline,'timeline','alt'),('08','Measurement',measurement,'measurement',''),('09','Execution',execution,'execution','alt'),('10','Downloads',downloads,'downloads','')]
footer=f'<footer><div class="wrap"><h3>Built on a clear source of truth.</h3><p class="note">Campaign judgments and budgets are planning recommendations. Company capabilities and event dates are grounded in the primary sources below, reviewed November 18, 2026. This is an independent campaign proposal, not a claim of festival affiliation or booked inventory.</p><ol class="sources">'+''.join(f'<li><a href="{url}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">{label}</a></li>' for label,url in sources)+f'</ol><p class="note">Official wordmark sourced from TerraFirma’s website. AI-generated illustrations are campaign concepts. Typesetting uses locally hosted, open-source Big Shoulders Display and Archivo.</p><a href="#top">↑ Back to the recommendation</a></div></footer>'
(OUT/'campaign.css').write_text(CSS)
(OUT/'index.html').write_text(head+'<body>'+header+'<main>'+hero+''.join(section(*s) for s in sections)+'</main>'+footer+'</body></html>')

